So you don't have to create and maintain a virtual machine/server yourself.

| Traditional Server           | Serverless                        |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| You manage the server        | Cloud provider manages the server |
| You configure infrastructure | Less infrastructure management    |
| Server may run continuously  | Can scale based on requests       |
| More server administration   | Focus more on application code    |


Simple example 🏠

Think of it like this:

Traditional server = You buy and maintain your own house. 🏠
Serverless = You use a managed hotel room; you use it, while someone else manages the building. 🏨

In one sentence

Serverless = Deploy your code or container, and the cloud provider manages the underlying servers and infrastructure.

====
Your Google Cloud Run deployment is serverless because you deploy your FastAPI application in a container, while Google manages the servers that run it.

====

Cloud Run is serverless because Google manages the underlying servers, while 
your Docker container still defines how the FastAPI application runs and scales with incoming requests.

